The canonical winding theorem (Corollary~\ref{cor:infinite-sheet-defect}, supplied by the resolution cluster) gives an infinite fiber for the canonical covering \(q\) before tilewise base change, and the exact fiber bijection of Proposition~\ref{prop:base-change}(5), iterated through the tower by Corollary~\ref{cor:kahn-tower}, preserves it.  Hence in any proposed whole-tile realization of \eqref{eq:resolved-factorization} the covering \(q\) has infinite fibers.  Since \(q\) is by typing a \emph{universal} covering, \(N=\operatorname{Deck}(q)\) acts simply transitively on each fiber, so \(N\) is infinite; in particular \(N\ne\{1\}\).  Theorem~\ref{thm:whole-tile-no-go} now denies the existence of the comparison.
